        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(agents): stop runs at the critical tool-loop threshold",
        "body": "Closes #106231\n\nAI-assisted: Codex implemented and reviewed this change under contributor direction.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where an agent that reached the critical tool-loop threshold would only emit a warning and continue running. The run could keep consuming tokens or repeating tool side effects until a separate timeout or limit stopped it, including through the embedded, Codex, and Copilot harness paths.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nA typed critical-loop signal now flows from the detector through each harness to one run-terminal owner. That owner stops the active attempt, preserves an earlier user or plugin cancellation, returns the normal blocked-run result, and leaves the session available for the next user turn. Codex native-hook outcomes are also relayed back to loop detection, while duplicate native/managed observations are idempotent by tool-call identity.\n\n## User Impact\n\nCritical tool loops now stop the current run at the configured critical threshold instead of continuing indefinitely. Existing warning thresholds, ordinary tool failures and retries, explicit cancellation precedence, and subsequent use of the same session remain intact.\n\n## Compatibility\n\nThis adds no config or storage surface. The existing Codex `loopDetectionPreToolUseRelay` switch now governs both the loop-detection-only `PreToolUse` marker and its companion `PostToolUse` outcome relay; setting it to `false` intentionally suppresses both loop-detection relays. Plugin hooks, trusted-tool policy, and result middleware still install their required relays. The reference and troubleshooting docs are updated to match.\n\n## Evidence\n\n### Redacted runtime proof\n\nRuntime proof was executed at `c7ca0bd6403e138e4c00b7eb11fdf5d8d842f69b` in an isolated temporary home/state/workspace against a local loopback OpenAI Responses stub. It used no AWS service, external model, external credential, or user data. Current head `345c592bf9b8f81ad7805daff60073c0f66fb91b` is a clean rebase: both commit patch IDs are unchanged, upstream touched none of this PR's files, and `git diff --check upstream/main...HEAD` passes.\n\nThe embedded OpenClaw CLI returned the following machine-readable outcome, then accepted a second turn with the same session id:\n\n```text\nplanned read calls = 3 (3 unique call ids)\nblocked text       = CRITICAL: Called read with identical arguments and identical outcomes 2 times. Session execution blocked to prevent runaway loops.\nlivenessState      = blocked\nerror.kind         = hook_block\nreplayInvalid      = true\nblocked session    = <same-session-id>\nrecovery text      = RECOVERED_SAME_SESSION_OK\nrecovery session   = <same-session-id>\n```\n\nA QA Lab runtime-pair scenario then exercised a real gateway child and qa-channel/qa-lab lifecycle for both `openclaw` and Codex app-server runtimes. Both cells passed. Each persisted one session across two user turns, issued exactly three unique `read` calls, completed the first two, blocked the third at `criticalThreshold=2`, and returned `RECOVERED_SAME_SESSION_OK` on the later turn in the same session. Codex recorded the third tool result as `isError=true` with `OpenClaw dynamic tool call aborted.`\n\nThe Codex cell exercises both the managed dynamic-tool bridge and Codex's native Pre/PostToolUse path: dynamic tools implement the default `CoreToolRuntime` hook payloads, and the registry runs those hooks around the call ([Codex dynamic handler](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/2895d82b5e449407712439ba4f89954f3fa0c7e3/codex-rs/core/src/tools/handlers/dynamic.rs), [Codex registry](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/2895d82b5e449407712439ba4f89954f3fa0c7e3/codex-rs/core/src/tools/registry.rs#L490-L603)). Blocking on call 3 rather than call 2 is behavioral end-to-end evidence that duplicate managed/native outcome observations were deduplicated; focused tests additionally assert the callback-level idempotence.\n\nThe proof-only mock trigger and QA scenario remained uncommitted in the disposable proof checkout. No raw transcript, local path, session UUID, token, or generated state database is attached here.\n\n### Deterministic and review proof\n\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/agents/embedded-agent-runner/run.critical-tool-loop.test.ts`: 7/7 passed. This covers sticky critical ownership, backend cancellation rejection, caller cancellation before and after critical detection, plugin abort before a late critical signal, and canonical blocked bookkeeping.\n- Focused coverage also covers threshold signaling, outcome ordering, duplicate native/managed observations, persistence, session adoption, usage/replay bookkeeping, Codex managed/native hooks, `/btw`, and the Copilot bridge.\n- Two independent source audits found no actionable defects after the cancellation, native-hook, side-question, and deduplication edge cases were addressed.\n- Two fresh `autoreview --mode uncommitted` passes found no accepted/actionable findings; the latest result was `overall_correctness: patch is correct` with confidence `0.97`.\n- The previous patch-identical GitHub Actions run passed build, type, lint, docs, security, QA smoke, and 79 test/check jobs. Its only primary failure was an unchanged `src/plugin-sdk/qa-runtime.test.ts` 25 ms real-clock boundary (`fetch` observed twice instead of once); the contributor account cannot rerun an upstream Actions job directly. Rebasing the unchanged patch onto current `main` has triggered a fresh CI run.\n\n### Dependency contract\n\nChecked directly against `openai/codex@2895d82b5e449407712439ba4f89954f3fa0c7e3`: [`success: false` dynamic-tool responses remain normal tool outputs](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/2895d82b5e449407712439ba4f89954f3fa0c7e3/codex-rs/core/src/tools/handlers/dynamic.rs), and native pre/post hooks share the tool call identity and response lifecycle ([registry dispatch](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/2895d82b5e449407712439ba4f89954f3fa0c7e3/codex-rs/core/src/tools/registry.rs#L490-L603)).\n",
